<h2>The Design Philosophy Behind the ISFDB</h2>
One of the first questions usually asked when someone looks at
the layout of the ISFDB is: "Why did you do it <i>that</i> way
instead of <i>this</i> way?" Here's a brief treatise on the
design philosophies behind the ISFDB:
<ol>
<li><i>Bibliographies are centered around people, not author names</i>.
It was decided fairly early on in the design phase that bibliographies
would be centered around the people who wrote the fiction, not the
(sometimes fictitious) names to which the work was ascribed. As such, authors
who use a large number of pseudonyms have all of their works listed in
a single location, denoted with pseudonym citations when necessary.
This was deemed especially necessary when working with house names (ala
<i>Will Garth</i>) in which several authors shared the same pseudonym.
<p>
As such, every author has a working name, around which the bibliographies
revolve. The process of selecting the working name is pretty loosely 
defined, but it's generally the name under which the author has produced the
majority of work. It's possible that some of these working names may not 
be the ones preferred by the author. Likewise, some 
bibliographic references may not reflect the actual name as published:
some publications are sloppy or inconsistent, listing (for instance)
<i>Brian Aldiss</i> instead of <i>Brian W. Aldiss</i>, or 
<i>Stephen Popkes</i> instead of <i>Steven Popkes</i>; in such cases the 
preferred working name is used to no ill effect. In other cases, such as: 
<i>Iain Banks</i> vs. <i>Iain M. Banks</i>, the distinction is important - 
in which case we use <i>Iain M. Banks</i> as the author's working name
(since that refers to his genre work), and <i>Iain Banks</i> as a pseudonym.
These changes may also cause an altering of the author name listed for
an award.  For instance, <i>Ursula K. Le Guin</i> is always used in an 
award listing, not <i>Ursula LeGuin</i>, or <i>Ursula Le Guin</i>, 
or <i>Ursula K. LeGuin</i>, all of which I have seen in various
awards listings.
<p>
<li><i>The focus of the ISFDB is bibliographic</i>.  We have no
interest in creating on online version of <i>Locus</i>.  Expansion
of the database is virtually certain, and in the future we may add 
genre tags (<i>Cyberpunk, Alternative History, etc...</i>), story 
synopsis support, expanded biographies, and maybe even a rating 
system.  But you won't find news, stories, reviews, press releases, 
interviews, or coverage of the WorldCon here. 
There's enough bibliographic work to keep an army of volunteers busy 
for years, without branching into other areas of the field already 
covered by professionals elsewhere.
<p>
<li><i>The focus of the ISFDB is on the written word</i>. 
While we do cover novelizations, 
nonfiction, selected non-genre works, and even game tie-ins, we 
don't cover extracurricular activities such as: games, movies, 
comics, trading cards, music, performance art, or stimsims.
<p>
<li><i>Bibliographic data is organized via publications, not authors.</i>
Our primary goal is to document all relevant publications in the field
of speculative fiction - not to create exhaustive author bibliographies.
The way that the data is organized in the ISFDB allows for data to be
easily displayed on a per-author basis, but it is not our desire to
collect ad hoc snippets of data for each author. Rather than recording
that author X contained story Y in collection X, we would much rather
record all of the stories contained in collection X. This not only adds
the appropriate data to author X's bibliography, but contributes to the
bibliographies of the other authors in the collection as well.
</ol>

